## Introduction
The goal of de novo protein design is the design of proteins with new catalytic, sensor, and information processing functions, as well as the elucidation of the principles of protein folding. The de novo design of proteins may be thought of as the construction of an amino acid sequence that folds back on itself in a designed (or at least in a defined) fashion to generate a molecule that exhibits cooperative unfolding based on tertiary as well as secondary structural interactions. This challenge can be recast as the problem of beginning with a backbone conformation and determining an amino acid sequence (or the manifold of sequences) that will fold into the desired conformation. For any protein to fold, its amino acid sequence must take advantage of hydrophobic, Van der Waals, and electrostatic interactions between amino acids to overcome the high chain entropy of the disordered amino acid chain and fold into a compact three-dimensional structure. An excellent way to imagine the problem and process of protein folding is through the use of energy landscapes [1•]. Each possible conformation of the amino acid chain is represented by a point in a multidimensional conformational space, with 3N-3 dimensions, where \(\mathsf{N}\) is the number of atoms in the protein. The multi-dimensional energy landscape of a protein is described by the free energy of each conformation of the protein as a function of the atomic coordinates. A deep funnel with a single free energy minimum represents a well-folded protein that can fold reversibly.
The scope of this review is an attempt to distill what are to the authors the most important considerations a protein designer must address, as well as principles that can be used to design proteins in general and helical proteins in particular.
## Binary patterning
## Proteins designed using patterning and inspection
Early reports incorporated the principle of binary patterning along with visual inspection to design helical proteins. In a series of papers, DeGrado and co-workers [14–16] described \(\alpha1\), \(\alpha2\), and \(\alpha4\), a four helix bundle protein designed incrementally. They first constructed an amphipathic \(\alpha\) helix (\(\alpha1\)) that was capable of self-association and then connected two and four of the peptides together with short turns (\(\alpha2\), \(\alpha4\), respectively). The component amphipathic helices of \(\alpha4\) consisted of leucines at roughly every 3.6 residues, and whereas the protein forms stable helices and is compact, it has properties that suggest that the residues in the core are not well packed. In a later report,
Handel et al. [17] demonstrated that incorporating a zinc binding site into \(\alpha4\) produced a protein with more native-like properties. Hecht et al. [18] described the design and characterization of the four helix bundle protein 'Felix', the protein incorporated binary patterning by laying the component helices out on helical net diagrams and hydrophobic residues were placed at 3.6 residue intervals. Helical net diagrams can be constructed by arranging the \(\alpha\)-carbons of amino acids as they fall on a helix onto a paper tube and cutting the tube lengthwise, followed by flattening the resulting rectangle. The arrangement of the \(\alpha\)-carbon positions represents the helical net. These early designs demonstrated the power of binary patterning to produce proteins that fold into helical bundles; however, in each case these proteins demonstrated properties consistent with a poorly packed core.
## Libraries of binary patterned proteins
## Packing
All protein designs must address the difficult problem of satisfactorily packing the protein core so that each amino acid side chain can pack into a unique environment. The simplest packing arrangement was suggested by Crick [21], in which two \(\alpha\) helices with hydrophobic side chains idealized by knobs can pack against each other, 'knobs into holes' packing, with the holes being the spaces between side chains. Packing this way, Crick predicted that the helixum would have a crossing angle of about \(20^{\circ}\). My coworkers and I [22] dealt with the packing problem in our design of a helical peptide that could pack against the hydrophobic surface of a transmembrane protein, by designing the primary hydrophobic interface consisting solely of alanines. We predicted that this hydrophobic alanine surface would pack against itself and observed that, in the crystalline-state, it indeed packs 'knobs into holes' with a crossing angle of about \(-20^{\circ}\) as an anti-parallel four helix bundle. In a subsequent report, we linked four of the helical peptides with glycine turns and demonstrated that the protein folded into a well packed structure \([4^{\bullet\bullet}]\).
