## Turns and helix termini
Nagi et al. \([34^{\bullet\bullet}]\) incorporated a series of turns consisting of 1–10 glycine residues in the protein ROP. They determined that all of the ROP turn mutants maintained their RNA binding activity and wild-type structure. The stability of the ROP turn mutants, however, decreased with increasing turn length in a way that correlated well with the free energy cost of closing the loop.
## Functional helical bundles
The design of functional proteins is challenging, but concrete examples are becoming available. Robertson et al. [36] constructed a 62-residue helix-turn-helix peptide that assembles into a four-helix dimer and binds heme through hydrophobic contacts and two histidine side-chains. Using the 'binary code' patterned four helix bundle library developed by Kamtekar et al. [19], Rojas et al. \([37^{\bullet\bullet}]\) have shown that half of the 30 proteins they expressed were capable of binding heme.
A development in protein engineering that has implications for design of functional proteins is a recent report of the engineering of cyclophilin into a proline-specific protease \([38^{\bullet}]\) . A Ser–His–Asp catalytic triad was introduced into the natural peptide-binding cleft of cyclophilin, converting it into endopeptidase that cleaved the natural substrate of cyclophilin.
## Conclusions
helical proteins the composition of turns is not a determining factor in the overall structure of the protein so long as the side-chains of the turn residues do not form specific contacts with the core of the protein. Finally, the length of turns should be kept short in order not to reduce the stability of the protein.
De novo protein design has reached a very exciting stage, where it is now possible to design small proteins that fold into predetermined three-dimensional structures. By combining the tools of de novo design, computational strategies of packing, combinatorial approaches to packing and binding, and ultimately the incorporation of functional determinants, the goal of de novo designing proteins with novel and useful functions is a reality.
